| A module speaking from a thread of its own.
|
| Every earlier inbound fixture pushes from inside the `code_module_dispatch`
| call it was asked on: still the program's thread, still inside the `emit`.
| That is a module answering elaborately, not a module with a life of its
| own. `test_timer` answers `Start` immediately and *then* pushes from a
| thread the program knows nothing about, one `Tick` per millisecond — which
| is what a terminal reading keys or a socket accepting connections actually
| does, and what the ring being locked is for (see `runtime.c`'s "Inbound"
| section and `native.rs`'s `InboundQueue`).
link "native_modules/test_timer.so" as timer

let seen = []

Tick { value } => {
    seen += value
    return Ack {}
}

emit Start { value = 3 } to timer get started
assert started.value = 3

| The event loop. Nothing in this body causes a push — the ticks arrive
| because a thread inside the module is putting them there while the program
| is somewhere else entirely, and the drain at the end of each iteration
| hands them over.
loop get spins = 0 {
    spins = spins + 1
    emit Length { value = seen } to core get n
    if n.value = 3 {
        break
    }
    | A bound, so a push that never arrives fails this fixture instead of
    | hanging it. Nothing about the passing run depends on the number: three
    | sleeps of a millisecond is over long before it.
    if spins > 2000000 {
        break
    }
}

| In order, and all three: the ring hands particles over oldest-first
| whichever thread put them there.
assert seen = [0, 1, 2]
